<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vĩnh Tế Canal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a>–<a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Vinh Te Canal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vĩnh Tế Canal is a canal of about 90 kilometres in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> delta, running along the Cambodian border from Châu Đốc to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Hà Tiên</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a>. Dug between 1819 and 1824 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Gia Long</a> and Minh Mạng by tens of thousands of Vietnamese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> labourers, it served defence, transport and irrigation, and it still marks much of the border.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vinh-te-canal.jpg" alt="Vĩnh Tế Canal" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bùi Thụy Đào Nguyên · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kinh_V%C4%A9nh_T%E1%BA%BF.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
